  ],
  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "CHAR and CODE use the Windows-1252 code page (CHAR(128) is U+20AC, astral characters give CODE 63), while UNICHAR/UNICODE use Unicode scalar values",
  "statement": "In Excel 16.0.20527.20088 (UI language 1033 / en-ZA), CHAR maps 1..=255 through Windows-1252 rather than ISO-8859-1, so CHAR(128) is '€' (U+20AC) and CHAR(159) is 'Ÿ' (U+0178). CODE(\"\") returns #VALUE!, and characters outside Windows-1252 such as an astral emoji (LEFT(UNICHAR(128512),1)) return 63 ('?'), whereas UNICHAR and UNICODE preserve full Unicode scalar values (UNICODE(LEFT(UNICHAR(128512),1)) = 128512).",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "Measured in Excel 16.0.20527.20088 across f2_rows (rows 1, 4) and dcfl2_text (rows 12-14) and matched across 1,056/1,056 CHAR, 2,109/2,109 CODE, 1,068/1,068 UNICHAR, and 2,087/2,087 UNICODE full-bank rows.",
  "inferred": null,
  "documentation": null,
